WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:06.760
When we talk about data centers, the
server rack is where it all begins.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.020
In the same way that a standardized
shipping container is a standard form

3
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.060
factor for everything we ship in the real
world, a server rack is a

4
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.560
purpose-built standard form factor for IT
equipment.

5
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:23.340
The most common size is a 19-inch rack.
The 19-inch

6
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.200
rack format has been around for decades,
and it remains essential

7
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.960
in today's IT infrastructure. The term
19-inch

8
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.100
refers to the horizontal width of the
rack, specifically the

9
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:38.700
distance between the mounting rails. This
is the

10
00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:42.760
standard form factor for rack-mountable IT
equipment. This

11
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.860
standard measurements of 19 inch, which is
about

12
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.020
48.2 centimeters, makes it possible to
mount

13
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.080
a wide variety of servers, switches, patch
panels, and other

14
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.400
IT equipment, even from different
manufacturers,

15
00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.560
all in the same rack. While the width is
standard,

16
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.020
it's worth noting that the rack depth can
vary on the equipment

17
00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:10.140
and use case. Data centers typically use
deeper racks to support

18
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.020
higher performance or dense compute gear,
while shallower depths

19
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:18.160
might be found in edge or telco
environments. But

20
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.220
racks don't just help with physical space.
They're an essential part of

21
00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.780
organization of equipment, maintenance,
airflow

22
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:30.800
management, and security. By vertically
stacking

23
00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.720
equipment in a rack, it helps you save
space and keep things

24
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.780
manageable. A key measurement you'll often
hear is a rack

25
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.760
unit or simply a U. One rack unit is

26
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:46.460
1.75 inches or approximately 44.45

27
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.960
centimeters in height. Most standard racks
are

28
00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.720
42U tall, meaning they can hold equipment

29
00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:57.760
that adds up to about 73.5

30
00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.680
inches or roughly 1.87 meters in total
height.

31
00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:06.460
IT equipment is typically labeled by its U
height,

32
00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.639
like a 1U server or a 2U

33
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.780
switch. But in the industry, we don't talk
about the physical height in

34
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.920
inches or meters unless we are shipping
the equipment.

35
00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.660
Typically, we label the equipment and
racks by their U height. The

36
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.560
larger the U size, the more capability or
function the

37
00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:30.180
equipment tends to have, but it also takes
