42
00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:49.980
and make the airflow more efficient.
Speaking of airflow,

43
00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:54.540
cooling is built into the design.
Equipment sucks

44
00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.360
cool air from the front and pushes warm
air out the back.

45
00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:02.880
That's why racks are often arranged in hot
and cold aisles.

46
00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:07.440
This separation allows facilities to
better manage temperature and

47
00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.500
energy usage. Mounting equipment is done
using rails along

48
00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:15.380
the sides of the rack. These rails have
holes spaced at standard

49
00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:19.260
intervals, so equipment can be locked in
with screws or quick-release

50
00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:23.000
tabs. This keeps everything secure and
aligned properly.

51
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:27.280
So why does standardization matter? Well,

52
00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:31.720
because it makes it easy to scale. Need to
add another

53
00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:35.300
server? You know exactly how much rack
space you have left

54
00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.760
and what size fits. That consistency
supports

55
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:43.020
both operations and future growth. Now,

56
00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:47.320
racks can also have doors, front and rear,
that are perforated to

57
00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.580
allow for airflow, but they also provide
security.

58
00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:55.040
In summary, a 19-inch rack may seem
simple,

59
00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:59.260
but it plays a crucial role in the data
center. It's the

60
00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:02.580
standardized form factor, and it's the
backbone

61
00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:07.260
of building blocks in the data center. It
ensures

62
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:10.840
compatibility, it enables organization, it

63
00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.680
improves cooling, and it supports
security, all while

64
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.079
